# LF in the working tree on every platform, not just in the repository. Several tests
# compare a COMMITTED text file byte-for-byte against text the build generates —
# hazard-scan-inventory.golden, ported-types.tsv, kit-slots.txt — and a CRLF checkout
# (Git for Windows defaults to core.autocrlf=true) fails all of them with a diff that
# prints identically, since \r is invisible. Measured on windows-latest, 2026-09-08.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Upstream licence texts are bytes we redistribute, not source we own: the BSD fork's LICENSE
# is CRLF as it came, and the retention clause is about that file rather than about a normalised
# copy of it. Git's content detection happens to leave it alone today; this makes that a rule.
third-party/**/LICENSE -text

# The other exception, and it is why the rule above has to be explicit rather than assumed:
# Windows batch scripts must keep CRLF line endings so they run when checked out on
# Windows (a plain LF .cmd misbehaves under cmd.exe). Last rule wins, so this stays below.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
